.TH MV 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
mv \- move files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mv
.RB [ \-f ]
.I source target
.br
.B mv
.RB [ \-f ]
.I source
.RI [ source ]\ .\|.\|.
.I target_dir
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mv
moves
.I source
to
.IR target ,
or, if more than one
.I source
is given (or a single
.I source
is given together with a
.I target
that already exists as a directory), moves each
.I source
into
.I target_dir
under its own final pathname component. It is an error for more than
one
.I source
to be given when the last operand does not name an existing directory.
.PP
Each move is attempted first as an atomic rename. If that fails
because the source and destination are on different filesystems or
volumes,
.B mv
falls back to copying
.I source
to
.I target
and then removing
.IR source .
Any other rename failure is reported as-is, with no fallback attempted.
.PP
When the cross-filesystem fallback is used and the source is a
directory, the whole tree is copied and then removed; if the copy
succeeds but the original tree cannot be fully removed afterward, this
is reported as a distinct condition (the destination now holds a
complete copy, but the source was left partially in place) rather than
as an ordinary copy or removal failure. When the fallback is used and
the source is a symbolic link, the move is refused with a diagnostic:
recreating a symbolic link's own target correctly across a
cross-filesystem move is not implemented, and copying the link's
referent instead would silently change what is being moved. This
limitation applies only to the cross-filesystem fallback; within a
single filesystem the atomic rename step, which is always tried first,
moves a symbolic link correctly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Accepted, but is a genuine no-op in this build:
.B \-f
's standard meaning is "do not prompt for confirmation before
overwriting", and this build never prompts (see
.B \-i
below), so there is nothing left for
.B \-f
to suppress.
.TP
.B \-i
Not implemented.
.B mv
prints a diagnostic and exits immediately with status 2 rather than
move a file without ever having prompted for confirmation.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I source
A pathname of a file or directory to move.
.TP
.I target
A pathname for the destination, used in the exactly-one-source form.
.TP
.I target_dir
A pathname of an existing directory, used when moving one or more
source operands into it under their own basenames.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every operand was moved successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred while moving one or more operands; the rest of the
operands were still attempted.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error occurred: an invalid option, a missing operand, a
missing destination operand, or more than one
.I source
given with a
.I target
that is not an existing directory. No moving is attempted in this
case.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cp (1),
.BR rm (1),
.BR ln (1)
